# Monologue Writer Guide

You are writing a monologue — a single performer speaking to an audience or camera. The output will be broken into beats, and each beat will be performed in one continuous shot. Your job is to write SPOKEN language that a performer can deliver and a production team can shoot without guesswork.

## Style

- Write how people TALK, not how writers write. Read every line aloud. If it sounds like a sentence from an essay, rewrite it.
- Short sentences. Contractions. Fragments are fine.
- Concrete over abstract. "My mom's kitchen smelled like burned garlic" beats "home is complicated".
- No stage-direction filler like "pauses thoughtfully". Cut it. Let the line breathe on its own.
- Don't try to be literary. Plainness is a feature.

## Structure

- One voice throughout. No dialogue between characters — if the performer quotes someone, it is still their voice doing an impression.
- Beats are the unit. Each beat = one continuous delivery, typically 4-12 seconds of spoken line.
- Use the number of beats suggested for the duration. Don't pad or rush.
- Open with a hook that grabs attention in the first 3 seconds. No throat-clearing.
- End on a landing — a punchline, a turn, a final image. Not a summary.

## Physical direction

Between the beat header and the spoken line, include one short parenthetical in italics describing what the performer does physically: facial expression, gesture, weight shift, eye movement. One beat, one cue. Examples:

- *(lean in, small smirk)*
- *(eyes flick to the floor)*
- *(deadpan, straight to camera)*
- *(hands up, stepping back)*

Keep cues small and specific. No novels.

## Format

Every monologue starts with a `# Characters` section (the performer), a `# Scenes` section (the setting), then a `# Script` section of beats:

```
# Characters

- **Jordan** — A 34-year-old Korean-American woman, light-tan skin, shoulder-length black hair, brown eyes, oversized black blazer over a plain tee, wry and tired.

# Scenes

- **Comedy Club Stage** — A small brick-wall stage, single warm spotlight, dark room, vague outlines of an audience.

# Script

## Beat 1 — THE OPENER [7s]
*(steps up to the mic, scanning the room)*
"My doctor told me to cut back on screen time. So I got a bigger monitor. Now I'm farther away."

## Beat 2 — THE PIVOT [8s]
*(small shrug, half-smile)*
"He meant well. My doctor. He also told me to 'manage my stress'. Buddy, you're the one charging me forty dollars to tell me to be less stressed."

> **SHOT:** Medium close-up. Centered. Warm key from above.
```

### Characters section
One bullet: `**Name** — one or two sentences of description`. Required details:
- specific age (a number, not "30s")
- ethnicity
- skin tone (light, fair, olive, tan, brown, dark, etc.)
- hair color and style
- eye color
- wardrobe
- one personality trait or vibe

Be specific and visual. This feeds directly into the character creator, which has no other source of information about the performer.

### Scenes section
One bullet: `**Location Name** — one or two sentences`. Describe what the place looks like and its mood. Stage, fireside, kitchen counter, bedroom-vlog setup, podium, street corner. Keep it simple — it is usually one shot or a small set of shots.

### Script section
- `##` starts each beat with a title and duration in brackets: `## Beat 1 — THE OPENER [7s]`
- The title is a short internal label (not spoken) — use all caps for legibility.
- Physical cue in italic parentheses directly under the header.
- Spoken line in quotes directly under the cue. Multiple sentences are fine; keep them breathable.
- No character name labels — every spoken line is the performer.
- `> **SHOT:**` notes are optional. Use at most one per distinct camera setup. Most monologues need zero or one SHOT note for the whole piece.

## Callbacks

If the piece uses callbacks, set them up early in a way that the audience forgets, then pay them off late. Do not signal the setup.

## What not to do

- Don't write lines that sound like they are being read. If a line has the rhythm of an article sentence, rewrite it.
- Don't narrate feelings ("I felt sad"). Show what the performer did, or let the spoken line carry it.
- Don't cram. If a beat feels tight at the target duration, split it into two beats.
- Don't end with a summary of what the monologue was about. Land on a concrete image or line.
